# XSH sem_close()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Closing an unnamed semaphore, using a closed handle and closing with
# blocked threads are undefined and not tried. When sem_open() returns the
# same handle twice, closing one would close both, so the test that needs
# two handles reports NOTINUSE. The case of a semaphore unlinked before the
# close is in sem_unlink.

assertion  XSH.sem_close.001
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       sem_close() of a handle from sem_open() returns 0.
test       t/001-close.c

assertion  XSH.sem_close.002
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       A named semaphore that has not been unlinked keeps its state
           across sem_close(): after two posts and a close, sem_open(name,
           0) succeeds and the value is 2 (sem_getvalue() and trywait).
test       t/002-state-kept.c

assertion  XSH.sem_close.003
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       With two distinct handles of one named semaphore, closing one
           leaves the other usable: a post through it works and trywait
           counts the semaphore's value (2 after a post on value 1).
test       t/003-other-handle.c

assertion  XSH.sem_close.004
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Where a named semaphore uses a file descriptor, sem_close()
           closes it; observable as an unchanged lowest free descriptor
           after twenty open/close cycles (after one warming cycle).
test       t/004-no-descriptor-left.c

assertion  XSH.sem_close.005
anchor     functions/sem_close.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       A child that opens a named semaphore, posts it once and closes it
           leaves the semaphore in place for the parent: the parent's handle
           still works and counts the child's post.
test       t/005-close-in-child.c
